.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt TIMER_CREATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timer_create ,
.Nm timer_settime ,
.Nm timer_gettime ,
.Nm timer_getoverrun ,
.Nm timer_delete
.Nd POSIX timers of a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn timer_create "clockid_t clockid" "struct sigevent *sev" "timer_t *timerid"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_settime "timer_t timerid" "int flags" "const struct itimerspec *new" "struct itimerspec *old"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_gettime "timer_t timerid" "struct itimerspec *cur"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_getoverrun "timer_t timerid"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_delete "timer_t timerid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn timer_create ,
number 259, makes a timer on the clock
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
or a clock of processor time:
.Dv CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID ,
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID ,
or the clock of a process that
.Xr clock_getcpuclockid 3
gives or of a thread of the caller's that
.Xr pthread_getcpuclockid 3
gives.
The timer is made disarmed, and its id, the lowest the process does not
have, is stored at
.Fa timerid .
.Fa sev
says how it is notified:
.Dv SIGEV_SIGNAL
posts the signal
.Fa sigev_signo
to the process,
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
to the thread
.Fa sigev_notify_thread_id
of the process, and
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
nothing; a NULL
.Fa sev
is
.Dv SIGALRM
with the id as its value.
The C libraries make
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
of
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
and a thread of their own.
A process has at most 64 timers.
.Pp
.Fn timer_settime ,
number 260, arms and disarms the timer as
.Xr timerfd_settime 2
arms a timerfd, with
.Dv TIMER_ABSTIME
for an absolute time, and
.Fn timer_gettime ,
number 261, reports its setting as
.Xr timerfd_gettime 2
does; the 64-bit time forms are numbers 409 and 408.
Times are kept in the same 10 ms ticks, rounded up, so a timer never
expires early.
.Pp
An expiration posts the signal with
.Va si_code
.Dv SI_TIMER ,
the timer's id in
.Va si_timerid ,
the value
.Fa sigev_value
in
.Va si_value ,
and in
.Va si_overrun
the expirations after the first that came while the signal was pending,
which go on adding to the pending signal until it is taken, as Linux
counts them.
While a signal of the same number that is not the timer's is pending, the
expirations wait and count toward the next signal.
.Fn timer_getoverrun ,
number 262, gives the overrun of the timer's last signal.
.Pp
.Fn timer_delete ,
number 263, disarms and deletes the timer.
.Xr execve 2
and the end of the process delete its timers; a child of
.Xr fork 2
has none.
.Pp
A timer on a clock of processor time counts the ticks the timer interrupt
charges to the process's threads, or to the one thread, as
.Xr times 2
counts them, user ticks alone for a clock of user time; its time left and
its interval are in ticks, and it expires at the tick that uses up the
last of its time, with no overrun, since each tick is counted.
A timer on another process's clock keeps counting that process until it
is deleted, and counts nothing once the process has ended.
.Pp
On x86-64 the timer calls take itimerspecs of 64-bit fields, and there
are no time64 forms.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn timer_getoverrun
returns the overrun, and the others 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
musl's
.Fn timer_delete
returns the negative error number itself.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EAGAIN
The process has 64 timers, or the kernel had no memory for another.
.It Er EFAULT
A structure is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
The clock, the notification or its signal is not one of those above, a
thread
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
names is not one of the process's,
.Fa timerid
is not a timer of the process, a flag is not
.Dv TIMER_ABSTIME ,
.Fa new
is NULL, or a time is negative or its nanoseconds are outside 0 to
999999999.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setitimer 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr timerfd 2
